[{:db/id "q-49008/selection/35b9f13cbdae0ff7da2e188ae82337d204d919d88fd144e8470cc7f8c5d2b727" :answer/id #uuid "edef131c-1a41-5809-9ba5-2388b4617cde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.6\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49008/selection/04d0a34eaf3f7675aeabc851fb3539a5563b06c2f6cde2d8dba3103a3f4d09fe" :answer/id #uuid "8d8b8820-1931-5088-ada3-0a64df6c20c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.25\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49008/selection/b46a9a5052960e50f71659d7917a35ea88248bff7c2e40209ce58891b2997e8a" :answer/id #uuid "1ae48c39-8212-58c1-a7c1-a4d99af765b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.85\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49008/selection/4440a4a5b412ee00c28b009f504b09fdedb0f221bbff2149847143e53e79538a" :answer/id #uuid "85048623-d94d-5fa8-9887-cbad1bb7fa0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.3\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49008/selection/3128854731866179655410d24e4d32564a0f0a2991f344b2be83c0fd0bc52eda" :answer/id #uuid "6d7d34de-f69c-5941-9ad5-b48b4e5087f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.7\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49008/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1dfd7748-4998-503e-a253-120bc977aca7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49008/selection/3128854731866179655410d24e4d32564a0f0a2991f344b2be83c0fd0bc52eda" :answer-field/choices ["q-49008/selection/35b9f13cbdae0ff7da2e188ae82337d204d919d88fd144e8470cc7f8c5d2b727" "q-49008/selection/04d0a34eaf3f7675aeabc851fb3539a5563b06c2f6cde2d8dba3103a3f4d09fe" "q-49008/selection/b46a9a5052960e50f71659d7917a35ea88248bff7c2e40209ce58891b2997e8a" "q-49008/selection/4440a4a5b412ee00c28b009f504b09fdedb0f221bbff2149847143e53e79538a" "q-49008/selection/3128854731866179655410d24e4d32564a0f0a2991f344b2be83c0fd0bc52eda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49008" :question/id #uuid "ea871d90-c900-55e4-a241-c287fbf062e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-49008" :question/problem "Ivan and Elisabeth have a date at the mall.\nIvan arrived at the agreed time, but Elisabeth is late. After $30$ minutes of waiting, Ivan decides to go home.\n\nIvan travels north from the mall with a speed of $4\\text{km/h}.$ Elisabeth travels west toward the mall. When Ivan is $1.2\\,\\text{km}$ from the mall, Elisabeth is $0.5\\,\\text{km}$ from the mall. If the distance between them increases at a speed of $1.5\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is Elisabeth's speed?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/2ddd52ba5c32fd8fa9b1a24ec9770bfda5103f61d365c49c13c1f52fbaa799bd.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Elisabeth and the mall,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Ivan and the mall,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Ivan and Elisabeth.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=0.5\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=1.2\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {0.5}^{2}+{1.2}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1.69 \\\\ z & =1.3\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=1.5\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0.5)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2(1.2)(4) & =2(1.3)(1.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3.9-9.6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Elisabeth is approaching the mall with a speed of $5.7\\,\\text{km/h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-49008/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49008"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-103781/selection/04c93ef2546fe5bd76780717b2182eb60230e1471bf6cc31d3db1921fc9d31de" :answer/id #uuid "55af6bd6-2482-5862-b1d2-858b92e04e04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103781/selection/de9dd8b1b1d5b3b34caf8bc025ac78de0354baf6f44c8c5f2a11917999ac6553" :answer/id #uuid "e5615bbb-907e-55da-be71-8d0c8b68c0fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103781/selection/dfc21ba0d09b7accb98af39644268ba7e0a14e6b2e6e6fa0ae58e4b5d9cfde57" :answer/id #uuid "ba77294a-ea2b-5f77-bdd3-8bd7422d2333" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103781/selection/042b7bb1c99fad3280281209915906a46e701054782d0226b6d5bd0584c7c92d" :answer/id #uuid "6cc1cb6b-e6e3-5bb7-89da-2d643b0efb2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103781/selection/1e87ad316333d0c713a8eb9bcc43e66e791eb329dcef7ae2c2725bb97b68c681" :answer/id #uuid "14ee404a-307f-5ba7-9321-4a08049d2319" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103781/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1419989e-abb4-572c-8291-f4575df1dbb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-103781/selection/042b7bb1c99fad3280281209915906a46e701054782d0226b6d5bd0584c7c92d" :answer-field/choices ["q-103781/selection/04c93ef2546fe5bd76780717b2182eb60230e1471bf6cc31d3db1921fc9d31de" "q-103781/selection/de9dd8b1b1d5b3b34caf8bc025ac78de0354baf6f44c8c5f2a11917999ac6553" "q-103781/selection/dfc21ba0d09b7accb98af39644268ba7e0a14e6b2e6e6fa0ae58e4b5d9cfde57" "q-103781/selection/042b7bb1c99fad3280281209915906a46e701054782d0226b6d5bd0584c7c92d" "q-103781/selection/1e87ad316333d0c713a8eb9bcc43e66e791eb329dcef7ae2c2725bb97b68c681"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-103781" :question/id #uuid "861245ce-e152-550e-a0d5-a98fa38203e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103781" :question/problem "A hot air balloon that is launched vertically is observed by a child located on the ground $4\\,\\text{km}$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the balloon at the instant when its distance from the child is $5\\,\\text{km}$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $12\\,\\text{km/h}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/40957ce27304474263d1d231a3a0df93193ac0461d8a7444cde3b12047d1a214.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the child and the launch site,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the launch site and the balloon,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the child and the balloon.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $z=5\\,\\text{km}$ and $x=4\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $y$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {4}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ y & =3\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=12\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the child and the launch site ($x$) remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(4)(0)+2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(5)(12) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =120 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{120}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical speed of the hot air balloon is $20\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-103781/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103781"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-313982/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "8a031a04-22f7-576a-bc5e-5f5e48c44e1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-313982/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "abd7da65-7704-5e74-b0ab-ab4ad4d4e595" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-313982/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-313982/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-313982" :question/id #uuid "d23bc7a2-a66d-5a97-8b57-920fdc962162" :question/math-academy-id "q-313982" :question/problem "A $15\\text{ ft}$ painter’s plank leans against a wall. The bottom end slides horizontally away from the wall at a constant rate of $3\\text{ ft/s}.$ How fast is the top end sliding down the wall at the instant when it is $9\\text{ ft}$ above the ground? Assume the rate at which the top slides downward is **positive**.\n\nThe top end is sliding down at a speed of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/afdb53c3134f18add3b07400805c76a36f3f7a6b8265a3f32e9e0dfaf1dd36e9.png)\n\nIn the diagram, \n\n- $x$ is the distance between the bottom of the plank and the wall,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the top of the plank and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the plank.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $y=9\\,\\text{ft}$ and $z=15\\,\\text{ft}.$ We can find $x$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{9}^{2} & ={15}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =225-81 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =144 \\\\ x & =12\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- The length of the plank remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(3)+2(9)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(15)(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{72}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the top end of the plank is sliding down at a speed of $4\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-313982/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-313982"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-2939/selection/d0e15931d1e7c73a9a588bdef312ae9a29520942e72ade8486ef79b0ec0a4c4e" :answer/id #uuid "30c44124-e9d8-5878-8bf7-f331ec526790" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60\\text{ km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2939/selection/e5c343b3584e166e0b5fb137d236a598a97feac360dc18ce46558283bb97f24e" :answer/id #uuid "64c1febb-03b2-55d4-a34c-0e6dd53133ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "70\\text{ km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2939/selection/eb61f8dc4086425c39740a3629a276f43fa2ab0e72028578e184974547520505" :answer/id #uuid "e7f79fc6-ff1e-57c4-89ff-0c38e9a02ed4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "75.2\\text{ km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2939/selection/9b7441e84eeddb9fb09e6b5650745b390dc359e6710e09f9b4f3984a035d211e" :answer/id #uuid "68d975c6-e233-54e6-afa7-cbfd38eba4ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "65.5\\text{ km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2939/selection/26de4d3a172365183f6002bde4a6179c4b153533dcc617731e2c9b7f9e87f3e2" :answer/id #uuid "a627611e-1de8-5fee-a092-69f8b0d865e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61\\text{ km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2939/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ccba75-b5f2-5edd-b43f-3b682b4639a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-2939/selection/e5c343b3584e166e0b5fb137d236a598a97feac360dc18ce46558283bb97f24e" :answer-field/choices ["q-2939/selection/d0e15931d1e7c73a9a588bdef312ae9a29520942e72ade8486ef79b0ec0a4c4e" "q-2939/selection/e5c343b3584e166e0b5fb137d236a598a97feac360dc18ce46558283bb97f24e" "q-2939/selection/eb61f8dc4086425c39740a3629a276f43fa2ab0e72028578e184974547520505" "q-2939/selection/9b7441e84eeddb9fb09e6b5650745b390dc359e6710e09f9b4f3984a035d211e" "q-2939/selection/26de4d3a172365183f6002bde4a6179c4b153533dcc617731e2c9b7f9e87f3e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2939" :question/id #uuid "507fbe2b-2f95-5bfa-be32-3403810daadf" :question/math-academy-id "q-2939" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/9283bcc77ac8e63c1ba00f9a81e659bcbcf41bc98b94a1a0617b02feab71cf0a.png)\n\nCar $B$ is being driven due south toward Caltech at a speed of $60\\,\\text{km/h},$ while car $A$ is being driven due east away from Caltech. When car $B$ is $0.6\\,\\text{km}$ away from Caltech, car $A$ is $0.8\\,\\text{km}$ from Caltech. If the distance between the two vehicles is increasing at a rate of $20\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is the speed of car $A?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/6e7ef0d4665710a31c1c601048498f20ad6b3099769c1d140320074805964ec7.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Caltech and car $A$,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Caltech and car $B$,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two cars.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=0.8\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=0.6\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {0.8}^{2}+{0.6}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =1\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-60\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=20\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0.8)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(0.6)(-60) & =2(1)(20) \\\\ 1.6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =40+72 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{112}{1.6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car $A$ is $70\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-2939/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2939"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344498/field-1/f9ce6d2531e925e028befc33f5bb5202bf5c98dc29df5818c58d5bd59c914412" :answer/id #uuid "95b3a29e-a085-5c5d-bd80-8430b253507e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344498/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5feedf8-aa34-5525-8c88-e94f493c76c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344498/field-1/f9ce6d2531e925e028befc33f5bb5202bf5c98dc29df5818c58d5bd59c914412" :answer-field/choices ["q-344498/field-1/f9ce6d2531e925e028befc33f5bb5202bf5c98dc29df5818c58d5bd59c914412"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344498" :question/id #uuid "ca2614df-5f7b-5ee7-bf5b-fa3d6af53b32" :question/math-academy-id "q-344498" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/c47df1761c64d428216092502779c45048dc68b98d8ecbc1f483aac484b0b67b.png)\n\nA car $B$ is driving due south toward an intersection at a speed of $60\\,\\text{km/h},$ while car $A$ is driving due east away from the intersection. When car $B$ is $12\\,\\text{km}$ from the intersection, car $A$ is $9\\,\\text{km}$ from the intersection. If the distance between the two cars is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is the speed of car $A?$\n\nThe speed of car $A$ is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{ }\\text{km/h}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/a89093949e721fffc50a2c8905ec8e081d45ce87e7ed3178a21121847dd67faf.png)\n\nIn the diagram:\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the intersection and car $A$\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the intersection and car $B$\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two cars\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know $x=9\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=12\\,\\text{km}.$ First find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {9}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 81+144 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =225 \\\\ z & =15\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that: \n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-60\\,\\text{km/h}$ (toward the intersection)\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=6\\,\\text{km/h}$ (increasing distance between cars)\n\nDifferentiate ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and substitute the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(9)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(12)(-60) & =2(15)(6) \\\\ 18\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1440 & =180 \\\\ 18\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1620 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of car $A$ is $90\\,\\text{km/h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344498/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344498"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-346032/field-1/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" :answer/id #uuid "24df65be-5d7c-5943-b7ad-b330fb8890c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-346032/field-1/e0e0a3ac4fe95f021c8f8aece7092270d36ad30c356733db1e628dc4f0b1d434" :answer/id #uuid "3fcbe045-2c3a-591a-b7d2-22485d99bf84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-346032/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "30e66efb-fe7f-56f9-8dde-0d6c44cec7df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-346032/field-1/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" :answer-field/choices ["q-346032/field-1/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" "q-346032/field-1/e0e0a3ac4fe95f021c8f8aece7092270d36ad30c356733db1e628dc4f0b1d434"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-346032" :question/id #uuid "c69063ca-2272-5fce-976c-d5f9e734d3d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-346032" :question/problem "Two snowplows are approaching a maintenance station $T$ along perpendicular roads. Snowplow $E$ drives due south toward $T$ at $24\\,\\text{mph},$ while snowplow $F$ drives due west toward $T$ at $10\\,\\text{mph}.$ When snowplow $F$ is $6\\,\\text{miles}$ from $T$ and snowplow $E$ is $8\\,\\text{miles}$ from $T$, how fast is the distance between the snowplows decreasing? Round your answer to the **nearest integer** and assume that the rate of decreasing distance is **positive**.\n\nThe speed of convergence of the two snowplows is approximately\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mph}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/1109f4736ddfa81344d545802438588967aa18aac379c75a6b7be58688fb931f.png)\n\nIn the diagram:\n\n- $x$ is the distance between snowplow $F$ and the maintenance station $T$\n\n- $y$ is the distance between snowplow $E$ and the maintenance station $T$\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the snowplows.\n\nBoth snowplows are moving toward $T$, so the leg rates are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{dt} & =-10\\text{ }\\text{mph} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dt} & =-24\\text{ }\\text{mph}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, compute $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 36+64 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =100 \\\\ z & =10\\text{ }\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and substitute the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\frac{dx}{dt}+2y\\frac{dy}{dt} & =2z\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ 2(6)(-10)+2(8)(-24) & =2(10)\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ -120-384 & =20\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ -504 & =20\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dz}{dt} & ≈-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSo, the speed of convergence of the two snowplows is approximately $25\\text{ }\\text{mph}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-346032/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-346032"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49007/selection/9f6e7e710eed4f19048bd956e9fb4ff8c3c8a198f67e327edca68c24853faa3a" :answer/id #uuid "d5299219-5c17-551b-81f9-0d5fbb1832c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36\\text{ ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49007/selection/951c12f6fe0ac0801c1ee4ea0eac35727b6530fb1affefb777b6e3d526a60453" :answer/id #uuid "b77fb926-7f27-5c11-8518-693d9926a170" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60\\text{ ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49007/selection/e7841587f888abb9028cd9f2f71efda968739a0b798d8320dd2cf6f6011f1c3e" :answer/id #uuid "77dc31ff-775c-54c6-ad62-819d139d7aaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32\\text{ ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49007/selection/51e959ffa2d4d7f12f521081cd9a971674b61269bdd70ae7f12ffcad31111074" :answer/id #uuid "8c81c336-2844-55b8-8da7-a3af65b90ba5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "66\\text{ ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49007/selection/3d08087877f50210e98916a4f8aa299ace7c37d45bc9212af93789024bb3c5c6" :answer/id #uuid "ebdb727b-9eac-5596-b394-af4a4b24af2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "56\\text{ ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49007/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c9da5ee-3602-51df-be37-f5ab5e2ee3c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49007/selection/e7841587f888abb9028cd9f2f71efda968739a0b798d8320dd2cf6f6011f1c3e" :answer-field/choices ["q-49007/selection/9f6e7e710eed4f19048bd956e9fb4ff8c3c8a198f67e327edca68c24853faa3a" "q-49007/selection/951c12f6fe0ac0801c1ee4ea0eac35727b6530fb1affefb777b6e3d526a60453" "q-49007/selection/e7841587f888abb9028cd9f2f71efda968739a0b798d8320dd2cf6f6011f1c3e" "q-49007/selection/51e959ffa2d4d7f12f521081cd9a971674b61269bdd70ae7f12ffcad31111074" "q-49007/selection/3d08087877f50210e98916a4f8aa299ace7c37d45bc9212af93789024bb3c5c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49007" :question/id #uuid "a77e15ea-ab18-5b8f-bc23-048daefb79b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-49007" :question/problem "A drone is released vertically from the ground at a constant velocity of $40\\text{ ft/s}.$ An observer stands $120\\text{ ft}$ away from the launch point. What is the rate of change of the distance between the drone and the observer $4$ seconds after the drone was launched?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/3f85af53794fa2f61a353f2e088cc1cecc9fc04f7453b97aa0ca808475e40035.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the observer and the launch point,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the drone and the launch point, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the observer and the drone.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the drone altitude after $4$ seconds. Since its velocity is constant, we get \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot t=40(4)=160\\text{ ft}\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {120}^{2}+{160}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 40\\,000 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =200\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=40\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- the distance between the observer and the launch pad remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(120)(0)+2(160)(40) & =2(200)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 12\\,800 & =400\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the drone and the observer increases at a rate of $32\\,\\text{ft/s}$ after $4$ seconds." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-49007/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49007"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-30080/selection/9e9a50e906d976ca6c901a74c1caa382e5e3db042ac4da00606c3e97f6d74b94" :answer/id #uuid "9079fd88-c4c7-53a5-9b1c-8c0bb299341b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\text{ ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30080/selection/fdfdd57fd5b69153a508ab9bccca2428363afc1b779aaa5ace32b3540bdad623" :answer/id #uuid "efc3f97c-296b-5986-b62c-9eaf4c0ca903" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\text{ ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30080/selection/6d4bf54e590899c3ad631db87cc69541e5680f98766e3e6ee4660f4f2214b93d" :answer/id #uuid "74332d96-fbda-56d8-ac95-5a2e5da09491" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28\\text{ ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30080/selection/4d2100a92fc6891cfc625b840d83e6a49357da31f77e2e7f331913adb331e126" :answer/id #uuid "4739f617-728e-5b4a-98a7-1bfb1f5e6d1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18.2\\text{ ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30080/selection/525257e350c84f9d974ecf16aa67ce0915ce32104a700b1eb367ec650484c291" :answer/id #uuid "94acd51e-40d6-56a4-bf4a-c5cf4a3ea6f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.2\\text{ ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30080/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "24d1fc14-9f68-5961-b4c9-1d96d16ba126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-30080/selection/525257e350c84f9d974ecf16aa67ce0915ce32104a700b1eb367ec650484c291" :answer-field/choices ["q-30080/selection/9e9a50e906d976ca6c901a74c1caa382e5e3db042ac4da00606c3e97f6d74b94" "q-30080/selection/fdfdd57fd5b69153a508ab9bccca2428363afc1b779aaa5ace32b3540bdad623" "q-30080/selection/6d4bf54e590899c3ad631db87cc69541e5680f98766e3e6ee4660f4f2214b93d" "q-30080/selection/4d2100a92fc6891cfc625b840d83e6a49357da31f77e2e7f331913adb331e126" "q-30080/selection/525257e350c84f9d974ecf16aa67ce0915ce32104a700b1eb367ec650484c291"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-30080" :question/id #uuid "a5414ba0-25e4-59ae-971e-c242926e55b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-30080" :question/problem "Two cars, $A$ and $B,$ are both initially at the point $O,$ and they both depart from $O$ at the same time. Car $A$ travels due east, and its distance from $O$ is given by $3t\\,\\text{ft}.$ Car $B$ travels due north, and its distance from $O$ is $2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{ft}.$ Here, $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. What is the rate of the change of the distance between the two cars after $2$ seconds?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/aa3f39f65b0785cfca2d1545e4bf331281f523f5e19777372dbb44096bdb4223.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between car $A$ and point $O$,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between car $B$ and point $O$, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between car $A$ and car $B.$\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the distance traveled by the cars after $2$ seconds. We get \n\n$$\nx=x(2)=3(2)=6\\text{ ft}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=y(2)=2(2)^{2}=8\\text{ ft}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 100 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =10\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe velocity of the cars is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(t)=3$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{′}(t)=4t.$ So, when $t=2$ their velocities are\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{′}(2)=8\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds,\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(2)=3\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds.\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(6)(3)+2(8)(8) & =2(10)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 164 & =20\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =8.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the cars is changing at a rate of  $8.2\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-30080/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-30080"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48953/selection/6e78a22664c68d65c58586ebc0e4402b3565972c8f4b02425a5e1be17716fd01" :answer/id #uuid "00ad3447-2192-57b6-887f-85dd9193168a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "144\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48953/selection/17cd5cb62d46fbc8654dde5086ed432cc0e3e6953da01db7b94fd5f83cc9c70b" :answer/id #uuid "f4415ae7-8ebc-524a-b7f4-f6b5bef8bbad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48953/selection/89e4d408f0b230aa092e7fb512d13c6caea5285046d72dd5d2b0289c46bff977" :answer/id #uuid "979e5140-bd9f-5472-ae49-8d2a366b3752" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48953/selection/482f35190f285ed64aa2e92b721b5c9a97a07ea05c684f8c099a60e74eee9875" :answer/id #uuid "501b1fcd-d219-53bc-899a-fd5dfa6553fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "96\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48953/selection/70ac1ab7e564277f2c8ee09973bdfc08d3dba7d780360c6ec72b0df2e44a7466" :answer/id #uuid "08751a96-8570-54c3-8e54-e0f1ac5669df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48953/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "311a83f5-4507-59e0-b813-3205814f9e31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48953/selection/70ac1ab7e564277f2c8ee09973bdfc08d3dba7d780360c6ec72b0df2e44a7466" :answer-field/choices ["q-48953/selection/6e78a22664c68d65c58586ebc0e4402b3565972c8f4b02425a5e1be17716fd01" "q-48953/selection/17cd5cb62d46fbc8654dde5086ed432cc0e3e6953da01db7b94fd5f83cc9c70b" "q-48953/selection/89e4d408f0b230aa092e7fb512d13c6caea5285046d72dd5d2b0289c46bff977" "q-48953/selection/482f35190f285ed64aa2e92b721b5c9a97a07ea05c684f8c099a60e74eee9875" "q-48953/selection/70ac1ab7e564277f2c8ee09973bdfc08d3dba7d780360c6ec72b0df2e44a7466"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48953" :question/id #uuid "0619c060-6164-5592-9cc0-f1974f0934ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-48953" :question/problem "Two buses, $A$ and $B,$ drive toward a school. Bus $A$ travels south at a constant velocity of $30\\,\\text{ mph},$ while the bus $B$ travels west at a constant velocity of $40\\,\\text{mph}.$ If bus $A$ is $16$ miles from the school, and bus $B$ is $12$ miles from the school, how fast is the distance between the buses decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram of the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/07eb0e89a4dc85160deb45ee13e6189d32bbc905bc638cb1aee891f0504ca950.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between bus $A$ and school,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between bus $B$ and school,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two buses.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=12\\,\\text{mi}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{mi}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =(12)^{2}+(16)^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =400 \\\\ z & =20\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-40\\,\\text{mph},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\,\\text{mph}.$\n\nNote that the rates of change are both negative because the distance to the school is decreasing for both buses. \n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(-40)+2(16)(-30) & =2(20)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 40\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-960-960 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1920}{40} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the buses is decreasing at a rate of $48\\,\\text{mph}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-48953/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48953"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-346050/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "94c5ef8a-5d90-5fab-be39-5d5a202da549" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-346050/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8cf9468-e5bc-565f-bdf9-40d5ded0b431" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-346050/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer-field/choices ["q-346050/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-346050" :question/id #uuid "f7c0283a-ec29-50bd-a4bf-7be6d57943ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-346050" :question/problem "A surveyor stands $15\\text{ ft}$ away from the base of a radio tower. An elevator inside the tower moves straight up at a constant speed of $20\\text{ ft/s}.$ How fast is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator changing when the elevator is $20\\text{ ft}$ above the ground?\n\nThe distance between the surveyor and the elevator is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/7a6f5b05bc0bd89d01b1b521fa35f4bc613e532a06ce04fea26323e6c5dbbafa.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the elevator and the ground,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=15\\,\\text{ft}$ and $y=20\\,\\text{ft}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {15}^{2}+{20}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 625 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =25\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=20\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(15)(0)+2(20)(20) & =2(25)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 800 & =50\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the surveyor and the elevator is $16\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-346050/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-346050"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-228"] :question/problem "A  $15\\,\\textrm{ft}$ ladder is leaning against a wall. If the top of the ladder is sliding down at a rate of $1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s},$ how fast is the bottom of the ladder moving away from the wall when the top of the ladder is $9\\,\\textrm{ft}$ from the ground?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/9e0fc4dc0e9ed182c4bfdcd3b127f9343fa6030be8c549c77880c6998a265c8b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the bottom of the ladder,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the ladder.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ \n\nWe know that $y = 9\\,\\textrm{ft}$ and $z=15\\,\\textrm{ft}.$ We can find $x$ using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{9}^{2} & ={15}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =225-81 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =144 \\\\ x & =12\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = -1\\,\\textrm{ft}/\\textrm{s},$\n\n- Note that the length of the ladder remains the same, so $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(9)(-1) & =2(15)(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{18}{24} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ladder is sliding away at a rate of $0.75\\text{ ft}/\\text{s}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-229"] :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/8c28dc5302e1f6e8e6ed905737b1487b7a2c6fcea7603c75eecca037ab64ebd1.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the radar station and the launch site,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the rocket and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the radar station and the rocket.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nWe know that $y = 15\\,\\textrm{km}$ and $x=8\\,\\textrm{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {8}^{2}+{15}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 64+225 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 289 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =17\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = 1700\\,\\textrm{km}/\\textrm{h}$ when $y=15,$\n\n- the distance between the radar station and the launch site ($x$) remains the same, so $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(8)(0)+2(15)(1700) & =2(17)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 51000 & =34\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =1500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the radar station and the rocket is increasing at a rate of $1500\\,\\textrm{km}/\\textrm{h}$ when the rocket is $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ from the ground." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
